2. WebSocket Streaming Quotas (Bronze Ingestion)

For live market data, Kite Connect uses binary WebSockets (KiteTicker) rather than REST polling:
• Concurrent Connections: Up to 3 WebSocket connections per API key.
• Instruments per Connection: Maximum 3,000 instrument tokens per WebSocket connection.
• Total Live Capacity: Up to 9,000 instruments simultaneously across 3 connections on a single paid API key (₹500/month).
• Streaming Modes:
	• ltp (Last Traded Price — lightest payload)
	• quote (OHLC, volume, buy/sell quantities, last trade)
	• full (Adds timestamp, OI, and 5-level market depth — required for options OI tracking and order book imbalance)

3. How This Impacts Your Medallion Architecture

• Fitting Your Universe into 9,000 Tokens: You have plenty of headroom on a single API key. Subscribing to all NIFTY 500 cash stocks (500 tokens) + NIFTY/BANKNIFTY spot & futures (~10 tokens) + ATM ± 15 strikes for index and active watchlist stock options (~1,500–2,500 tokens) uses only ~2,000–3,000 tokens—fitting inside 1 to 2 WebSocket connections.
• Bronze Backfill Bottleneck (3 req/sec): Pulling 7 years of 1-minute historical candles via Kite's Historical API at 30 days per call requires ~85 requests per symbol. For 500 stocks, that is ~42,500 calls (~4 hours of continuous rate-limited fetching). Note: Kite Historical API does not provide expired options contracts—you must capture live option chain ticks into your own Bronze layer daily or source expired options data from an external vendor (e.g., Truedata, Global Datafeeds, or NSE Bhavcopy/tick archives).
• SEBI 10 OPS Threshold: Operating at or below Zerodha's 10 orders per second cap keeps your individual algo within standard retail API execution limits without triggering exchange high-frequency algo registration requirements.

